{
 "id": "2021-ho-street",
 "name": "Street Performance and Public Space Perception",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Study 1 with the original images: participants first see all 12 public-space images of Hong Kong (transport facility, street, square, recreational space, found neighborhood space, park, memorial, market, playground, community open space, indoor marketplace, waterfront) without street performance, in random order. After a definition of street performance, each participant is randomly assigned one space type and sees its animated image, in which a guitar-playing busker and two passersby fade in and out, with the space type's definition and the paper's instruction to imagine using the place regularly. They then rate how much the street performance changes the place, from -3 (Much less) to +3 (Much more), on the paper's 13 items for visitability (4), restorativeness (5) and preference (4), mixed in random order with four instructed-response attention items. Gender and age follow. Rebuilt from Ho & Au (2021), Effect of Street Performance (Busking) on the Environmental Perception of Public Space, Frontiers in Psychology 12: 647863, with the 12 GIF images from its Supplementary Appendix A (CC BY 4.0; built on Ho & Au's 2020 public-space images) and the items from its Table 4. Media ids are the space types; 'space_type' records the assignment. Analysis as in the paper: composite change scores (visitability, restorativeness, preference), one-sample t-tests against 0 for all spaces and for each space type, keeping only participants who answer all four attention items correctly; the paper found busking raised all three (preference +0.78 overall), except at the memorial. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper did not print its question stem or its exact busking definition shown to participants; the stem is ours and the definition is the paper's own definition from its introduction; (2) the paper did not say which answers its four attention items asked for; ours ask for Much less, Slightly more, About the same and More; (3) the opening set of 12 images is shown as one page; (4) demographic questions are ours (the paper also asked location, education and class); Study 2 (on-site in Hong Kong) is not included.",
